<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">al-Maqrizi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1364">1364</a>–<a href="/years/1442">1442</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Maqrizi was an Egyptian historian of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> period and a pupil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a>, who held posts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> including that of market inspector. His Khitat, a topographical history of Cairo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and his chronicles of the Fatimid, Ayyubid and Mamluk dynasties are among the most important sources for the history of medieval Egypt.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Architectural patronage by Qalawun, al-Nasir Muhammad, Sultan Hasan and later Qaitbay made Cairo a showcase of Islamic architecture, while scholars such as Ibn Khaldun, <b>al-Maqrizi</b> and Ibn Hajar made it a centre of learning.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>By <b>al-Maqrizi</b>'s account Umar died around 1275 and 'four or five sons' ruled briefly until Sabr ad-Din I held power to the turn of the century; his successor Sultan Ali was the first to war with Abyssinia and soon submitted again for want of popular support.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-ifat">Sultanate of Ifat · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The polymath Abd al-Latif al-Baghdadi (1163–1231) measured it and praised it as a work of engineering genius, and <b>Al-Maqrizi</b> (1364–1442) noted that it was a grave.</blockquote><a href="/work/great-pyramid-of-giza">Great Pyramid of Giza · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>The historian Ibn Taghribirdi (1411–1470), son of the amir Taghribirdi, studied under <b>al-Maqrizi</b> and wrote a great chronicle of Egypt and its sultans, al-Nujum al-zahira.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mamluk-sultanate">Social Hierarchy of the Mamluk Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cairo, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. Died in Cairo, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.04,31.24&amp;year=1414" aria-label="Cairo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5361 1067 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of al-Maqrizi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5811 1337h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5811 1337h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=30.04,31.24&amp;year=1414">What was here in 1414? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
